PMB OS patch: execve redirect through $PMBOS_LOADER (Android "loader in jniLibs" exec mode)
Upstream: glibc 2.36 as shipped by Debian bookworm, source package glibc 2.36-9+deb12u14
          (pinned: GLIBC_DEBIAN_VERSION in versions.env = debian_version in
          pmbos/tools/loader/VERSION), after Debian's quilt series and after
          glibc-2.36-pmbos-rootfs.patch; also applies to plain glibc-2.36.tar.xz.
Apply:    patch -p1 --fuzz=0 < glibc-2.36-pmbos-execve.patch   (stages/stage-glibc.sh)

Why: with targetSdk >= 29 Android forbids execve() of files in the app data dir (SELinux: no
execute_no_trans on app_data_file), so imagefs programs (box64, dash, ...) cannot be exec'd.
execve() of the APK's extracted native libraries and mmap(PROT_EXEC) of app data files are
still allowed. The APK ships Debian's stock ld.so of the SAME glibc version as
<nativeLibraryDir>/libpmbld.so (pmbos/tools/loader); the launcher starts
  libpmbld.so --library-path <libdirs> --argv0 <argv0> <imagefs>/usr/bin/box64 ...
and every glibc program those processes start must go through the loader as well.

What: sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/execve.c replaces the execve syscall stub of syscalls.list
(__execve, weak execve - the entry point of execv/execvp/execvpe/execl*, posix_spawn(p),
system, popen and wordexp inside libc). With PMBOS_LOADER unset it is exactly the stub. With
PMBOS_LOADER=<absolute ld.so> (taken from envp, else from the caller's environment; ignored
for AT_SECURE) it calls sysdeps/unix/sysv/linux/pmbos-execve.h, which rewrites
  execve(PATH, [ARG0, ARG1...], ENVP)
for a dynamically linked glibc program of this machine (ELF of the libc's class/byte
order/e_machine, ET_EXEC/ET_DYN, PT_INTERP basename "ld-linux*") into
  execve($PMBOS_LOADER, [$PMBOS_LOADER, "--library-path", $PMBOS_LOADER_LIBPATH,
         "--argv0", ARG0, PATH, ARG1...], ENVP)
("--library-path" only if PMBOS_LOADER_LIBPATH is non-empty, "--argv0" only if ARG0
exists, PATH as "./PATH" if it has no '/' or starts with '-'). "#!" scripts are parsed like
Linux binfmt_script (256 bytes, interpreter + one optional argument, ENOEXEC rules); a
missing absolute interpreter becomes $PMBOS_ROOTFS<interp>; the interpreter is then handled
the same way with [INTERP, OPTARG, PATH, ARG1...] (up to 4 nested scripts, then ELOOP).
Static programs, other loaders (Android's /system/bin/linker64), foreign machines, other
formats, non-regular/unreadable/missing files and files the caller may not execute
(access X_OK; ld.so itself does not check) use the plain syscall. EACCES for an
executable regular file in no known format is reported as ENOEXEC (what Linux says when
SELinux does not refuse first; keeps execvp's /bin/sh fallback). If the new vector would
exceed the kernel's pointer limit (min(6 MiB, RLIMIT_STACK/4), >= 128 KiB) -> E2BIG before
anything is allocated. Async-signal- and vfork-safe (posix_spawn's child): no malloc, stdio
or locks; fstatat/open/pread/close/access/getrlimit/execve only; vectors via alloca.

Not covered (documented): execveat() and fexecve() (direct execveat syscall), raw
syscall(SYS_execve), /proc/self/exe of a redirected program (= the loader; ld.so fixes argv
and AT_EXECFN). No rtld/ld.so change and no struct/ABI change: the stock Debian ld.so of the
same version works with this libc.so.6.

Unit test: src/glibc-execve-hosttest.c (tests/execve-redirect-hosttest.sh extracts
pmbos-execve.h from this patch); integration test: src/glibc-execve-test.c (stage glibc,
with the rebuilt and with the stock pinned loader).

